WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.600
To be a futurist you have to declare
yourself to be a futurist

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:08.280
within some community of futuristic practice.

00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:11.160
It's not a thing you can do all by yourself.

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:15.080
So for instance to be a science-fiction brand of futurist

00:00:15.080 --> 00:00:17.960
I have to publish some science fiction.

00:00:17.960 --> 00:00:21.080
If I just write my weird musings about the future

00:00:21.080 --> 00:00:24.040
in my private diary nobody's ever gonna know.

00:00:24.040 --> 00:00:26.720
It's gonna make zero difference.

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:29.540
I have to participate in some kind of publishing system.

00:00:29.540 --> 00:00:32.900
I have to be involved in the literary world in some way.

00:00:32.900 --> 00:00:34.540
And I have to communicate.

00:00:34.540 --> 00:00:36.680
Otherwise I'm the Crusoe guy,

00:00:36.680 --> 00:00:39.880
telling himself bar stories alone.

00:00:39.880 --> 00:00:43.520
So for me to write a novel about the future though

00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:48.600
somebody has to invent the literary concept

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:52.700
of writing a novel with a setting which is in the future.

00:00:52.700 --> 00:00:57.300
And that did not happen until the 1600s.

00:00:57.300 --> 00:00:59.940
I mean people were writing stories for millennia

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:01.500
before anybody thought

00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:04.920
'why don't I just set it in the time to come'

00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:08.280
somebody had to and undertake this literary invention.

00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:10.180
It had never occurred to anybody.

00:01:10.180 --> 00:01:12.800
It was a kind of Copernican breakthrough, it's like

00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:14.940
'wait, I could set it in the past
or I could set in today...

00:01:14.940 --> 00:01:17.400
or I could like set it in the future!'

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:23.540
And this happened like 10 or 12 times by different authors before anybody really caught on.

00:01:23.540 --> 00:01:25.400
And thought you know this could work.

00:01:25.400 --> 00:01:29.240
It was independently invented by a large variety

00:01:29.240 --> 00:01:31.160
of Robinson Crusoe-style

00:01:31.160 --> 00:01:34.540
"Ur-science fiction" writers who

00:01:34.540 --> 00:01:37.760
wrote these eccentric things and nobody picked it up

00:01:37.760 --> 00:01:38.760
nobody went anywhere with it.

00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:42.140
There was no futuristic community of practice.

00:01:42.140 --> 00:01:45.260
They would just write it and have it go on.

00:01:45.260 --> 00:01:50.460
It was impossible at that time to be futuristic 
in the science fictional way.

00:01:50.460 --> 00:01:52.100
You just couldn't do it.

00:01:52.100 --> 00:01:56.160
Until people had a discourse of scientific romance.

00:01:56.160 --> 00:01:59.520
A way to talk about it.

00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:04.180
And they had a publishing structure, and
a distribution structure, and an audience,

00:02:04.180 --> 00:02:06.220
and some literary cliques.

00:02:06.220 --> 00:02:08.620
And also some critical responses.

00:02:08.620 --> 00:02:15.380
And before those things were invented you just 
couldn't do it. It was just beyond the capacity.

00:02:15.380 --> 00:02:19.240
But after they were invented, then
you could be a science fictional futurist.

00:02:19.240 --> 00:02:23.980
Mostly through declaring to other people
that you were one.

00:02:24.640 --> 00:02:27.480
And they had to hear you.

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:28.980
And they had to agree.

00:02:28.980 --> 00:02:31.740
They had to validate your declaration.

00:02:31.740 --> 00:02:33.220
It's like, yeah, you're one of us.

00:02:33.220 --> 00:02:36.900
Here's your Hugo Award. You know, whatever.

00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:39.180
And you could join the
Science Fiction Writers of America.

00:02:39.180 --> 00:02:42.260
But without them, you're the Robinson Crusoe guy

00:02:42.260 --> 00:02:44.800
drawing weird diagrams.

